#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222365} :activity/id #uuid "3f795870-787b-5622-b078-947df7d3cd17" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048235 :topic/id #uuid "8b0ed26c-25c0-57a8-8d1c-6baa9735f5e3" :topic/math-academy-id 6373} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222365 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222492 :tutorial/content "We have seen how, for an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a) = c,\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates a solution by evaluating the slope at the current point,\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = y' \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwhile the implicit Euler method approximates a solution by evaluating the slope at the next point,\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = y'_\\text{new} \\cdot \\Delta x.\n$$\n\nBoth have their advantages, but is there a way to combine them into one numerical method, applying the slope at both the current point and next point to update $y?$\n\nYes, there is! One such method is the trapezoidal method, which takes the *average slope in the interval between $x$ and $x_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x.$*\n\nMore formally, the **trapezoidal method** with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\dfrac12(y' + y'_\\text{new}) \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwith $y'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nLike the implicit Euler method, the trapezoidal method is an implicit numerical method. To apply the method, we substitute the formula for $\\Delta y$ into the update formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{'}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{'})\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}(f(x,y)+f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve the resulting implicit equation for $y_\\text{new}$ directly, rather than explicitly calculating the value of $\\Delta y$ beforehand.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll focus on IVPs whose slope function is linear in $y,$ i.e., of the form\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = a(x)y + b(x).\n$$\n\nIn such cases, we can simply solve the implicit equation for $y_\\text{new},$ as demonstrated in the following worked example.\n\nFor more complex slope functions, we require a root-finding method, such as Newton's method, to solve the implicit equation iteratively. We'll cover this in a later lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "a1419612-b51a-5051-9478-0b5686cac1cd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14490 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "fc3f4329-6227-53b8-a848-08d6504d86a7" :step/math-academy-id 600181 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222366}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222366 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222493 :tutorial/content "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = x + y + 1, \\qquad y(0) = 2\n$$\n\nLet's use the trapezoidal method with one step to approximate the value of $y(1).$\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 1 - 0 = 1.\n$$\n\nThen, in general, the change $\\Delta y$ for our ODE is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{'}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{'})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+y+1+({x}_{\\text{new}}+{y}_{\\text{new}}+1))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+y+{y}_{\\text{new}}+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute this into $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+y+{y}_{\\text{new}}+2) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y+{y}_{\\text{new}}+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for the general formula for $y_\\text{new}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y+2)+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y+2) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is $x_\\text{new} = 0 + 1 = 1.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = 0 + 1 + 3\\cdot2 + 2= 9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1) \\approx 9.$ We can view our approximation made by the trapezoidal method in the coordinate plane as follows:\n\n![](images/a2/a233830a75e76788ed9c4a5436ecb237aad6257591f0e20768c796195dca8eb2.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "c20c0100-eb93-50ee-b684-c56f4c9ec02d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14491 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "30729e81-2455-57d6-9939-77e4c4541d91" :step/math-academy-id 600182 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222367}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222367 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221437 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221179 :question/id #uuid "62a1e90b-05a5-5828-abe0-fa5cbb4c1c1a" :question/math-academy-id "e-21672"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "78599a22-23ff-5b95-8305-fc40fd3bf548" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050084}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221967} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308646} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308647} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308648} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308649}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining a General Update Formula for the Trapezoidal Method"} :step/id #uuid "e6615bd4-7ab0-53c0-94f6-6ac2b5d6f381" :step/math-academy-id 600183 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222368}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222368 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221438 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221178 :question/id #uuid "5399454b-0691-5cb3-884f-26af4ca52f62" :question/math-academy-id "e-21671"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ccbf6e21-8dcb-5237-8c31-2a499858f973" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222009} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222010} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314842} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314843} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314844} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314846} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314876} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372726} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372732} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372738}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Step Using the Trapezoidal Method"} :step/id #uuid "c9aae869-52a0-5af1-bbbd-93e447dc18db" :step/math-academy-id 600186 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222405}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222369 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221439 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221180 :question/id #uuid "d387bb78-9df1-5551-9e1e-5a4745c5b731" :question/math-academy-id "e-21673"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe0a8062-1387-5dd7-a568-0b0a6c4d04c6" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222015} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222016} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314868} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314869} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314870} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314871} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314872} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314873} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314874} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314875} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372744} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372753}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Steps Using the Trapezoidal Method"} :step/id #uuid "250a7a8e-0521-5bf0-ad50-c0c51ae3eba8" :step/math-academy-id 600189} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222405 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222532 :tutorial/content "Once the first iteration of a trapezoidal method implementation is complete, we can continue applying the same general update formula iteratively.\n\nIn our previous example, we used one iteration of the trapezoidal method on the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = x + y + 1, \\qquad y(0) = 2,\n$$\n\nusing the general formula\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = x + x_\\text{new} + 3y + 2\n$$\n\nto approximate the value $y(1)\\approx9.$ Let's now approximate the value of $y(2)$ with one more step.\n\nSince the step size $\\Delta x = 1$ remains the same, the update formula derived in the previous step still applies.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have $x=1$ and $y=9.$ Hence, the new $x$-value is $x_\\text{new} = 1+1=2,$ and the new $y$-value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =1+2+3\\cdot 9+2 \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2) \\approx 32.$ We can visualize this second step as follows:\n\n![](images/cd/cd9752c16b15ebcfcfa214c775e99fb68139a1dfa9364a5f552fbf676d7953f7.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "31f14503-26a9-5e09-b9cf-10a636f52db7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14898 :tutorial/title "Multiple Iterations of the Trapezoidal Method"} :step/id #uuid "b71ca947-77a6-59f8-9d4b-6ed77ec28832" :step/math-academy-id 608074 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222369}}] :activity/title "The Trapezoidal Method" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186221465}]}
